/debug
string role = "guest";
if (role = "admin") {
    cout << "Access granted";
}

Use `==` to compare. A single `=` assigns "admin" to `role`, and a `string` can't be used directly as a true/false condition, so the compiler reports an error. (With an `int`, the same mistake would compile and always be true.)

```cpp
if (role == "admin") {
    cout << "Access granted";
}
```

How would you write the opposite check, for anyone who is not an admin?

/debug
string name = "Fluffy";
string &ref;
ref = name;

Initialize the reference at the time of declaration using `string &ref = name;`. References cannot be declared and then assigned later like pointers can.

```cpp
string name = "Fluffy";
string &ref = name;
```

What happens to `name` if you change the value of `ref`?

/debug
void heal(int h) { h += 10; }
int hp = 50;
heal(hp);
cout << hp;

Change the parameter to a reference: `void heal(int &h)`. This tells C++ to pass the actual variable instead of a copy, allowing the function to modify `hp` directly.

```cpp
void heal(int &h) { h += 10; }
int hp = 50;
heal(hp);
cout << hp;
```

What would be the syntax to achieve the same result using a pointer instead of a reference?
